{% set local_problem %}
<p>Madison homeowners: if your home was built between 2000 and 2015, your gutters were probably the cheapest option your builder could find. That's not a criticism — it's just how spec housing works. Builders buy gutters by the truckload, and they buy what meets code, not what performs best.</p>

<p>The result? Thousands of Madison homes with 5-inch sectional gutters, .027 gauge aluminum, and hangers spaced at the code-minimum 32 inches. For the first five years, these systems work fine. Then the seams start separating, the hangers pull loose (especially after ice loads), and the undersized capacity shows up every time a spring storm drops 2 inches in an hour.</p>

<p>We've been replacing builder-grade gutters in Madison subdivisions for 15+ years — Clift's Cove, Bradford Creek, Mill Creek, Dublin Park area, the neighborhoods along Highway 72. The pattern is the same everywhere: homes reach the 7-10 year mark and the gutters start failing.</p>

<p><strong>The subdivision drainage reality:</strong> Madison lots are graded to drain into each other and toward retention ponds. That means your property might be receiving runoff from multiple uphill neighbors. Your gutters need to handle your roof's water <em>plus</em> whatever your soil is already dealing with from neighboring drainage. Undersized gutters make this worse.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Seamless gutters are fabricated from a continuous roll of aluminum coil — formed on-site by a portable machine we bring to your driveway. Instead of pre-cut 10-foot sections joined with seams, you get continuous runs with connections only at corners and downspout outlets.</p>

<p><strong>What we actually install:</strong> 6-inch K-style gutters in .032 gauge aluminum. Hidden hangers at 24-inch intervals (tighter than the 32-inch code minimum). 3x4 downspouts positioned every 35-40 linear feet of gutter run. Everything measured and fabricated to your home's exact dimensions.</p>

<p><strong>Why the specs matter:</strong> Six-inch gutters hold roughly twice the water of five-inch. The .032 gauge aluminum is about 20% thicker than .027 — it doesn't dent when you lean a ladder against it, and it holds shape better over time. The 24-inch hanger spacing prevents the sagging you see on older systems where hangers are 32 inches apart.</p>

<p><strong>The honest bit:</strong> "Seamless" doesn't mean zero seams. You still have seams at inside corners, outside corners, and downspout connections. What you don't have is a seam every 10 feet in the middle of a run — and those mid-run seams are what actually fail first on sectional gutters.</p>
{% endset %}

